[Alice]: Alright everyone, let's dive into the claim that Albert Einstein failed his mathematics class. The truth is, this is a myth that has been perpetuated over the years. In reality, Einstein excelled in mathematics from a young age. He reportedly scored a 6 out of 6 in his mathematics exams at the Polytechnic Institute in Zurich.

[Bob]: Yeah, but I think failing a math class is just a way to say he struggled at some point, right? I mean, everyone has ups and downs in school. Plus, it makes for a great story!

[Charlie]: Hold on a second, Bob. While it’s true that struggles are common, let's focus on the facts. According to a 2018 article published by the "Journal of Educational Psychology," there's no evidence of Einstein ever failing any mathematics class. Instead, he was known for his exceptional mathematical skills, even in high school.

[Dave]: But that’s exactly what they want you to believe! I read somewhere that the education system back then was rigged. They didn’t want geniuses like Einstein to shine too brightly! You can’t trust those studies; they’re all part of a cover-up!

[Eve]: Dave, while I appreciate your passion, let's stick to verifiable facts. The documentation of Einstein’s academic performance is available in historical archives. For instance, his transcripts from the Polytechnic Institute show consistent high marks. I can pull that up using Python if we need to analyze the data.

[Frank]: It’s important to note that Einstein did have a rocky start in his early education, particularly with authority figures. He was expelled from one school, but that was for disciplinary issues, not academic failure. His math skills were never in question.

[Grace]: So, it sounds like Einstein had some challenges, but maybe we can agree that he was still an exceptionally gifted mathematician? It’s a bit of a gray area, isn’t it?

[Alice]: I appreciate the attempt at a compromise, Grace, but the facts are clear. Einstein was more than competent in mathematics. He was already developing theories that relied heavily on complex mathematical principles well before he graduated.

[Bob]: But wouldn’t it be more relatable if he had struggled a little? It’s kind of inspiring to think that even someone as brilliant as Einstein faced challenges like the rest of us!

[Charlie]: That may be true, but wanting the narrative to fit a certain mold doesn’t change the historical facts. The myth of Einstein failing math is more about how we perceive genius than about his actual academic performance.

[Dave]: Narrative or not, I still think there's something fishy about how history is presented. They don’t want us to know the real truth about geniuses and their struggles. I mean, have you ever considered how many other great minds faced similar issues?

[Eve]: Speculating about hidden truths doesn’t help us here. We need to focus on the documented evidence. For example, Einstein published his first paper on special relativity in 1905, which he could only have done with a strong grasp of advanced mathematics.

